{"name":"Lime Sorbet Green","hex":"#c6cd7d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lime","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any inorganic material containing calcium, usually calcium oxide (quicklime) or calcium hydroxide (slaked lime).","Any gluey or adhesive substance that traps or captures; sometimes a synonym for birdlime."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To treat with calcium hydroxide or calcium oxide (lime).","To smear with birdlime.\n To ensnare, catch, entrap."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lime"},{"term":"sorbet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Frozen fruit juice, sometimes mixed with egg whites, eaten as dessert or between courses of a meal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sorbet"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sorbet","blocks":[{"text":"Sorbet is a frozen dessert made using ice combined with fruit juice, fruit purée, or other ingredients, such as wine, liqueur, or honey."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Strawberry_sorbet_zoomed.jpg/330px-Strawberry_sorbet_zoomed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sorbet","wikidata":"Q13302"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"},{"title":"Lime (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Lime is a color that is a shade of yellow-green, so named because it is a representation of the color of the citrus fruit lime. It is the color that is in between the web color chartreuse and yellow on the color wheel. Alternate names for this color included yellow-green, lemon-lime, lime green, or bitter lime."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/AosaHGkyuai_%28cropped%29_3.jpg/330px-AosaHGkyuai_%28cropped%29_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lime_(color)","wikidata":"Q906936"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Magnolia_Sorbet_from_the_Elora_Mill_Hotel_Restaurant.jpg/500px-Magnolia_Sorbet_from_the_Elora_Mill_Hotel_Restaurant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Magnolia_Sorbet_from_the_Elora_Mill_Hotel_Restaurant.jpg/1280px-Magnolia_Sorbet_from_the_Elora_Mill_Hotel_Restaurant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_conten